{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-334193",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-4}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $({x}^{2}-3)^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot 0=0\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
